═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
FIELD INTERPRETATION GUIDE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
- "building" [Operations]: from sheet name pattern ("BUILDING A"), or a
  "Subject:" header cell. If unknown, use the sheet name verbatim,
  confidence "low".
- "trade" [Operations]: from a "Scope:" header cell, or inferred from
  file/sheet name. If inferred rather than explicitly stated, confidence
  "medium" or lower.
- "weightPct" [Finance]: always normalize to 0-100 scale. See Output Rule 12.
- "accToDatePct": only extract from a clear "% Complete" / "To-Date %" /
  "Accomplishment %" column. Never guess from narrative text. Always
  normalize to 0-100 scale (see Output Rule 12).
- "level"/floor: only fill if explicitly named (Ground Floor, 2nd Floor,
  Podium, Roof Deck, Basement, Mezzanine, Penthouse). Leave null otherwise.
- "isMilestone": set true only when the source explicitly labels the row as
  a Milestone / MS, or the row has plannedStart === plannedFinish AND the
  source itself indicates zero duration. Do not infer it from a short
  duration alone.
- Section-header rows (no dates, no amounts) inform category/subcategory for
  rows beneath them — they do not become their own records. [Operations]
- Dates wildly outside a reasonable construction timeframe (e.g. year 1900 or
  2099) usually mean an uncalculated spreadsheet formula — still output the
  record with confidence "low", note it in dataIssues, and log it as
  UNUSUAL in meta.structuralFindings. Don't silently fix it.
- "costEntries.type" [Finance]: "committed" means a PO/contract is issued
  but not yet paid — use this only if the source distinguishes it from paid
  actuals (words like "committed", "PO issued", "encumbered"). If the source
  only has a simple budget/actual split, use "actual" for anything already
  paid and "budget" for planned figures — do not invent a "committed" value.
  amount may be positive or negative.
- "violations": look for NCR logs, safety inspection reports, QA/QC checklist
  flags, non-compliance notices. "severity" should only be filled if the
  source explicitly grades it (Critical/Major/Minor or equivalent wording) —
  otherwise "unspecified". Do not resolve status yourself — see What Not To
  Do.
- "quantityTakedowns" [Operations]: look for Bill of Quantities (BOQ),
  material takeoff sheets, or drawing-quantity schedules — distinct from
  materialEntries (which track stock/consumption); takedowns track measured
  quantities of work/material required per scope.
- "forecastNotes": look for narrative sections in progress reports, PM
  reports, minutes of meeting, or risk registers that discuss what MIGHT
  happen (delays, overruns, shortages) rather than what already happened.
  Do not convert a forecast into a task or cost entry — keep it as a
  narrative risk record. Only fill "quantifiedImpact" if a specific number
  or day-count is explicitly stated in the source; otherwise leave "".
- "variationOrders": use this array for any Variation Order / Change Order
  document or line item, instead of folding it into costEntries. If
  "amountDelta" is not explicitly stated as a number, leave it null rather
  than estimating from narrative description.
- "rfis": use this array for Request for Information logs. Set
  "impactsScheduleOrCost" to true only when the RFI text or its answer
  explicitly states a resulting change; otherwise null (not false — false
  would imply you confirmed no impact, which you usually cannot).
- Common contractor report types to recognize and route correctly:
  · Weekly/Monthly Progress Report → tasks (accToDatePct) + forecastNotes
  · S-Curve / Physical Progress Report → tasks (weightPct + accToDatePct)
  · Manpower Report → note in meta.blindSpots (no dedicated array in this
    schema version); do not force into materialEntries or costEntries
  · Delay Notice / Extension of Time (EOT) request → forecastNotes
    (topic: schedule_delay) + relevant tasks if specific activities named
  · Variation Order (VO) → variationOrders (not costEntries)
  · RFI (Request for Information) → rfis if it has a subject/date/status you
    can extract; meta.skippedSections only if there is truly nothing
    structured to capture
  · Minutes of Meeting → forecastNotes for any risk/delay discussion +
    violations for any non-compliance items raised
  · NCR / Non-Conformance Report → violations (type: quality_ncr)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WHAT NOT TO DO
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
- Do not average, total, or calculate anything not already in the source.
- Do not fabricate a p6ActivityId, resourceCode, sourceRowRef, or loggedBy if
